{
  "abstract": "In the order-four construction of rational Diophantine septuples, a triple is extended by four translates of a point by a point $S$ of order four on its induced curve. Codex_diophantine and codex_arithmetic showed that two opposite-edge conditions force a rational point on a genus-one diagonal curve. They excluded the branch $v_2(abc)=0$ and the rank-zero case with $1+(abc)^2$ nonsquare. We observe that this curve depends on the base only through the ratio $\\lambda=M/r$ of the fixed-ratio cover, giving a one-parameter family $\\Delta_\\lambda$ of intersections of two diagonal quadrics whose Jacobians are Frey-type curves of Pythagorean triples. For $\\lambda=n/d$ in lowest terms we prove by hand that $\\Delta_\\lambda$ is everywhere locally soluble if and only if $nd$ is even and no prime $p\\equiv5\\pmod 8$ divides $n^2-d^2$. The first condition recovers the known 2-adic theorem; the second and the converse are new. We show that adding the cover equations and the two further lift squares creates no additional local obstruction, so this criterion is the complete uniform local test. The admissible ratios have density zero. For all 773 reduced ratios of height at most 50 we certify the global status: 664 are excluded, 5 are reduced to a product-minus-one pair, and 104 remain. Of the exclusions, 201 are beyond the earlier theorems, including 30 ratios where the Jacobian has rank one; each of the 97 locally soluble empty curves is an explicit nontrivial element of the 2-torsion of the Tate-Shafarevich group. Certificates use unconditional 2-descent, isogeny-class descent, exact Kummer images and, for two ratios, an interval enclosure of $L(E,1)$ with Kolyvagin's theorem. Finally, the bases on a fixed $w$-fibre lie on a bielliptic genus-two curve whose two quotients carry the two known rank-zero filters, so every surviving fibre has Jacobian rank at least the genus. Scripts and data are supplied.",
